-**Parameters and Edge Cases:**
-*   **Chunk Boundaries:** Adjacent cubes across chunk boundaries (e.g., x=0 and x=15 in a 16-wide chunk) must be checked. If ignored, the outline of each chunk is drawn, killing performance [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Bottom Faces:** In the specific implementation cited in [S2, unofficial], bottom faces are always culled (`renderFlag = false`) regardless of neighbors below. This is a design choice, likely to hide the terrain floor or simplify collision logic, and is not a universal rule for all face culling methods.
-*   **Side Face Logic:** In the specific implementation cited in [S2, unofficial], side faces are also culled if a neighbor exists directly above the neighbor position. This suggests a specific occlusion model where overhangs or adjacent upper blocks hide side faces, which deviates from standard geometric face culling. Implementers should verify if this "above neighbor" check is required for their specific visual style or if it is an artifact of the cited source's specific game logic.
-*   **Precision:** Bitmask operations require careful handling of bit width to avoid overflow or incorrect neighbor mapping in 3D grids.
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Chunk Boundaries:** Adjacent cubes across chunk boundaries (e.g., $x=0$ and $x=15$ in a 16-wide chunk) must be checked. If ignored, the outline of each chunk is drawn, killing performance [S1, unofficial].
+- **Bottom Faces:** In some implementations, bottom faces are always culled regardless of neighbors below, as indicated by logic where `isBottomFace` sets `renderFlag = false` [S2, unofficial]. This is a specific optimization choice, not a general rule of face culling.
+- **Side Face Logic:** Some implementations check for neighbors directly adjacent OR neighbors directly above the neighbor position to cull side faces, which may be a specific heuristic for terrain generation [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Binary Hidden-Face Culling
-A technique to reduce the number of faces in a voxel mesh by removing those completely hidden (adjacent to another voxel), using bitwise operations on a bitmask representation of voxels to determine visible faces in bulk [S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from the hash-set neighbor check by operating on contiguous memory blocks, avoiding random memory access penalties associated with looping through neighbors [S3, unofficial]. Choose this method when performance is critical and the voxel data can be efficiently linearized into bitmasks. Pitfall: The original approach of checking each of the six faces by looping through neighbors and performing random memory access leads to very poor performance [S3, unofficial].
+### [Binary Hidden-Face Culling](8747-binary-hidden-face-culling.md)
+Uses bitwise operations on linear bitmasks to identify exposed surfaces in bulk, replacing random memory access with parallel bitwise math. Choose this when processing large, contiguous volumes where bitwise parallelism outweighs the complexity of mask management.
+
+### [Face Coverage Mask Culling](3397-face-coverage-mask-culling.md)
+Uses coverage masks to determine visibility. Choose this when dealing with partial voxel occupancy or non-cube shapes.
+
+### [ChunkMesh Vertex Packing and Management](3566-chunkmesh-vertex-packing-and-management.md)
+Manages vertex buffers for chunks. Choose this when optimizing GPU memory bandwidth and draw calls for chunked terrain.
+
+### [Voxel Backface Culling and Internal Face Masking](4569-voxel-backface-culling-and-internal-face-masking.md)
+Culls backfaces and internal faces. Choose this when rendering opaque voxels and camera position is known.
+
+### [Block Visibility Culling](5334-block-visibility-culling.md)
+Culls entire blocks based on visibility. Choose this for very sparse or large-scale scenes.
+
+### [Extraction of Surface Points (Sobierajski)](5721-extraction-of-surface-points-sobierajski.md)
+Extracts surface points for meshing. Choose this when generating meshes from implicit surfaces.
+
+### [Mask Projection Culling](6836-mask-projection-culling.md)
+Uses projection masks. Choose this for view-dependent culling.
+
+### [Mesh Culling via 3D Projection and Connected Components](6963-mesh-culling-via-3d-projection-and-connected-components.md)
+Uses projection and connected components. Choose this for complex, disconnected terrain.
+
+### [Opacity-based Mesh Culling](8759-opacity-based-mesh-culling.md)
+Culls based on opacity. Choose this for transparent or semi-transparent voxels.
+
+### [From-Region Visibility Preprocessing](9048-from-region-visibility-preprocessing.md)
+Preprocesses visibility. Choose this for static scenes with known camera regions.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(V)$ where $V$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel's 6 neighbors are checked once. With bitmasks, this can be optimized to $O(V/64)$ or better using SIMD/bitwise parallelism.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ to store the voxel data and the resulting mesh. The mesh size is significantly smaller than the naive $6V$ faces, often reducing triangle count by a factor of 1000 in homogeneous terrain [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** With this optimization working correctly, performance allows running more than 14,440 chunks on a fairly old PC (Intel Core i7 2.5 GHz, AMD Radeon HD 6770M 1024 MB) while performing GPU-heavy algorithms like cascaded shadow mapping and water shading [S1, unofficial]. Without it, only ~20 chunks could be drawn without lagging [S1, unofficial]. Another measurement cites 4096 cubes rendered at ~60 FPS on the author's system [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Drastically reduces GPU draw calls and triangle count. The culling check runs once during mesh creation, resulting in no framerate loss during rendering [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of chunk boundaries to avoid visible seams [S1, unofficial]. The specific logic for bottom and side faces in [S2, unofficial] may not suit all game genres (e.g., caves or overhangs).
-*   **When to use:** Ideal for voxel games with large, homogeneous terrains where static or semi-static mesh generation is feasible.
-*   **When not to use:** Not suitable for highly dynamic environments where voxels change every frame, unless the mesh regeneration is optimized (e.g., using bitmasks).
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of solid voxels, assuming $O(1)$ hash set lookups. The culling check runs once during mesh creation, resulting in no framerate loss during rendering [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the hash set and vertex buffer.
+- **Measured Performance:** With this optimization, performance allows running more than 14,440 chunks on a fairly old PC (Intel Core i7 2.5 GHz, AMD Radeon HD 6770M 1024 MB) while performing GPU-heavy algorithms like cascaded shadow mapping and water shading [S1, unofficial]. Without it, only ~20 chunks could be drawn without lagging [S1, unofficial]. Another measurement shows 4096 cubes rendered at ~60 FPS [S2, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** Performance scales linearly with the number of chunks, provided the hash set lookups remain efficient.
+- **Strengths:** Dramatically reduces triangle count (drawing only ~1/1000 of the triangles previously drawn in homogeneous terrain) [S1, unofficial]. Simple to implement.
+- **Limitations:** Requires a full world or chunk neighbor lookup, which can be complex in chunked systems if boundary checks are ignored [S1, unofficial]. Not suitable for dynamic scenes where voxels change frequently without re-meshing.
+- **When to use:** Homogeneous voxel terrain where most faces are occluded.
+- **When not to use:** Sparse voxel clouds where most faces are exposed, or dynamic scenes requiring frequent mesh updates.
 
 ## Background
-Face Culling in voxel rendering originated as a fundamental optimization in early voxel games to make large worlds renderable on limited hardware. It is widely used in voxel game implementations alongside VBO per chunk, frustum culling, and backface culling [S1, unofficial]. The technique is often referred to as "Hidden-Face Culling" in modern devlogs [S3, unofficial].
+Face Culling is a standard optimization in voxel rendering, widely used in voxel games and terrain generation systems. It was discussed in the context of optimizing voxel world rendering on Game Development Stack Exchange [S1, unofficial] and implemented in the Voxel-Terrain-Generation repository [S2, unofficial]. The technique is fundamental to reducing the GPU load in voxel engines by eliminating interior geometry.
 
